 "productionNotes": "No single production total for the 1964-1974 GTO is asserted here, because the year figures do not agree to the car. The New Hampshire GTO Association's factory tables and the Standard Catalog of Pontiac figures reproduced by Muscle Car Club give 32,450 for 1964, 75,352 for 1965, 96,946 for 1966, 81,722 for 1967, 87,684 for 1968, 72,287 for 1969, 40,149 for 1970, 10,532 for 1971, 5,807 for 1972, 4,806 for 1973 and 7,058 for 1974, which add to 514,793. Wikipedia gives 75,342 for 1965, and 5,811 for 1972 with the note that some sources omit one convertible and three wagons to reach 5,807. The variant counts are where the arguments are. The 1971 Judge is 357 cars in Wikipedia and 374 in the club tables and in Curbside Classic's notes to the Car Life test; the club tables reach 374 by adding 357 hardtops and 17 convertibles. The 1969 Ram Air IV is 759 cars in Curbside Classic, Jalopnik and the club tables, while a CarTech restoration text gives 549, which the club tables show as the manual-transmission hardtop count alone. For 1970 the club tables list 3,629 Judge hardtops and 3,615 plain convertibles; Muscle Car Club prints the same two numbers the other way round. Wikipedia counts 18 Ram Air IV Judge convertibles for 1970, six of them automatics, against Hagerty's seven automatics and ten four-speeds. Every one of these is a count of factory billing records as republished by clubs and writers; none of the sources consulted here reproduces a Pontiac document, and none reconciles the others.",
